# 0077-nas-one-level-of-subfolders.patch -- the NAS source also lists the game files of the sync folder's DIRECT subfolders (one level, never deeper), so games kept one per folder
# ("games/<game>/<file>.nsz") show in the library, the Cloud page and the Build tab like the ones in the folder itself.
# Applies after 0076. UNTESTED ON DEVICE, IN THE SIMULATOR AND AGAINST A REAL SFTP SERVER: compiled only, and its pure part ran in a host check (below).
# Regenerated in place on 2026-11-21 for review r4 §4.5 (REVISED, below); 0078 applies after it.
#
# WHY (the owner; his log)
#   * The owner's sync folder was ".../games" and the games live in subfolders of it; Nas.List read the folder alone (one level), so the NAS listed none of them. He asked for one level of
#     subfolders, no deeper, to keep the listing light. The owner's example, ".../games/<collection>/<game>",
#     is TWO levels down: this patch does not reach it with "games" as the sync folder; the owner chooses ".../games/<collection>" (or the game's own
#     folder) as the sync folder instead, which the folder chooser allows (below).
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   * GameAppNas.cs, NasScan (pure, no SSH: compiled by the host check with NAS_LOGIC_ONLY, which leaves out `using Renci.SshNet` and the Nas class):
#       - one list call for the sync folder, then one per subfolder, in name order (OrdinalIgnoreCase), at most MaxSubfolders = 200 per listing; past the cap the rest is left out and logged
#         ("NAS: <dir> has 250 subfolders: only the first 200 (by name) were looked into; choose a narrower sync folder to see the others"). A subfolder's own folders are never listed.
#       - looked into (LooksInto): real folders only. Never a symbolic link (the SFTP listing reports the link itself; a link back to the folder or above would list it again or loop), a
#         hidden folder (".x"), a NAS system folder (@eaDir, #recycle, #snapshot, @Recycle, @Recently-Snapshot, @__thumb, .@__thumb: Synology puts @eaDir in every folder), "Exports"
#         (any case, like the system folders: NasScan.IsExports), or the folder itself.
#       - kept: the sync folder's own files exactly as before (TopFile: a game or an .ipa, not .part); Exports' .ipa files as before; in a subfolder (SubfolderFile) game files only (.nsp .xci
#         .nsz .nup), not hidden ("._Game.nsp" from a Mac), not a link, not .part.
#       - each CloudFile: SourceId the NAS's, Name the file's own name (the game's name, the name it gets in Documents), Size from the listing, Remote the full path the server gave
#         (SftpFile.FullName): Nas.Download and Nas.WithReadStream (the covers) read that path, nothing else changes there.
#       - name clashes (the same file name in two places; Documents holds one file of a name, and the library and DownloadSet match by name): the first stays, in this order: the sync
#         folder's own files, Exports, then the subfolders by name; every other one is skipped and logged ("NAS: skipped <path>: a file of that name is already listed (<kept path>), the first
#         one is kept"), at most 10 lines, then "… and N more".
#       - a subfolder (or Exports) that cannot be listed is skipped and logged ("NAS: could not list <path>: <why> (skipped)"); the sync folder's own listing failing still throws, as before
#         (RefreshCloud catches it and shows "Could not refresh: …").
#       - the summary line, which replaces "NAS: N game or .ipa file(s) in <dir>": "NAS: 12 game files in /path and its 7 subfolders" (" and 2 .ipa files" when there are some); the
#         subfolders counted are the ones READ (one that could not be listed is named on its own line, not counted).
#   * Nas.List uses NasScan with the real lister (client.ListDirectory -> NasEntry), on the caller's thread: RefreshCloud's worker (Task.Run), as before; no new main-thread work. A folder
#     "/" no longer becomes "" when its trailing "/" is trimmed.
#   * GameAppLibrary.cs, RefreshCloud: Nas.List gets a log (posted to the main thread's Write, as the connection test does) instead of null, so the summary, the cap, the clashes and the
#     unreadable subfolders reach the log. This also logs Connect's "NAS: connected to user@host:port (SHA256:…)" at each NAS refresh.
#   * Update and DLC grouping: unchanged code. Cloud.CloudOnlyGames, Cloud.DownloadSet, GameAppUi.UpdateFor / DlcFor work on file names and title ids across the whole NAS source, so a
#     game's update and DLC are found in its own subfolder (and in any other listed place of the NAS); the download set carries each file's real Remote path.
#   * The folder chooser (BrowseNas): it already went down any number of levels ("›") and offered "Use this folder" at every level; it showed only the first 30 folders of a level, so a game's
#     folder past the 30th (a games folder holds one folder per game) could not be chosen. It now shows up to 200 (NasFolderRows; the action sheet scrolls), and its message says "The games in
#     this folder and in its direct subfolders are listed."
#   * GameAppCloud.cs: the header comment only.
#
# REVISED (2026-11-21, review r4 §4.5: three low findings on 0077)
#   * "Exports" was excluded from the game folders, and found for its .ipa files, with StringComparison.Ordinal while the NAS's own folders used OrdinalIgnoreCase: an "exports" folder was
#     listed as a GAME folder (its .ipa skipped, its game files listed). Now both use NasScan.IsExports (OrdinalIgnoreCase); when two folders differ only by case, the exact "Exports" is the
#     one read for .ipa files (else the first by ordinal name), and none of them is a game folder.
#   * NasScanResult.Subfolders counted the ATTEMPTS (a subfolder whose listing failed was counted), while the summary says "its {Subfolders} subfolders". Subfolders now counts the ones
#     read; the new Looked counts the attempts, and Capped is SubfoldersFound > Looked (a failure is not a cap).
#   * The cap is on subfolders (one list call each), not on files: every game file of a subfolder looked into is listed, and so is every file of the sync folder. That is the intended
#     semantics (the cap bounds round trips); the texts now say so: MaxSubfolders' comment, the cap's log line ("... were looked into (the limit is on subfolders, not on files: each one
#     looked into has all its games listed); choose a narrower sync folder ...") and the folder chooser's message ("The games in this folder and in its direct subfolders (the first 200 by
#     name) are listed.").
#   * Verified after the revision (2026-11-21, M1 Pro): the same compile recipe (a full `dotnet build gameapp.csproj -c Release -r ios-arm64` of a copy of the device project, sources of this
#     tree): 0 errors, 41 warnings, the same warnings per file and code as the 0077 of 2026-11-20 (the count differs from the 40 below: the device project copy now has one more file of its
